---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Rawan Aladdin — For Robots & Pencils"
  description="A targeted mini-portfolio: user research, design systems, and shipped AI product work by Rawan Aladdin, for the Robots & Pencils UX/UI Designer role."
  bodyClass="rp-pitch"
  canonical="https://rawanaladdin.com/robots-and-pencils"
  noindex
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/robots-and-pencils.css" />
  </Fragment>

  <main>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ HERO ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="hero" class="rp-section" style="min-height: 100vh; justify-content: flex-end; padding-bottom: 8rem;">
      <p class="rp-hero-label">For Robots &amp; Pencils — August 2026</p>
      <h1 class="rp-hero-h1">Hi Robots &amp;<br>Pencils, I'm Rawan.</h1>
      <p class="rp-hero-sub">
        A designer who starts with the person and stays with the work through build —
        user research, design systems, and the interfaces that carry them.
      </p>
      <div class="rp-hero-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rp-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ WHY ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="why" class="rp-section">
      <p class="rp-section-label">Why me, for this role</p>
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Three things<br>I'd bring.</h2>

      <div class="rp-ladder">

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">01</p>
          <h3>I've designed an AI product, not just an interface for one.</h3>
          <p>
            On Onward, the hard problem was never the model. It was the moment its
            output landed on someone who was already nervous. That is the same problem
            as an agent sitting inside a person's actual workflow — the thing has to be
            useful to them at the moment it speaks, or it is a demo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">02</p>
          <h3>I build design systems that survive production.</h3>
          <p>
            For Feeds I wrote an eleven-page system and then applied it into the product
            myself, in the engineer's codebase, through his code review. A system nobody
            applies isn't a system — it's a PDF. Being the one who lands it means I know
            what a component costs before I propose it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">03</p>
          <h3>I research first, then decide quickly.</h3>
          <p>
            Interviews and surveys, a persona, a how-might-we, three design pillars — and
            then, because the timeline was tight, a decision to make one flow flawless and
            let the rest follow. Speed doesn't come from cutting the research. It comes
            from knowing which decision the research is actually for.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rp-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════ PROJECT 01 — ONWARD ═══════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="onward" class="rp-section">
      <p class="rp-section-label">Project 01 — Shipped AI product</p>
      <div class="rp-project-head">
        <h2 class="rp-project-title">Onward</h2>
        <span class="rp-meta">Product Designer &amp; Lead Developer · Sept – Dec 2024</span>
      </div>
      <p class="rp-lede">
        An AI interview coach for internationally educated healthcare professionals —
        nurses, mostly, arriving into a hiring system that expects them to interview in
        a register nobody teaches them. I designed it and led the build.
      </p>

      <div class="rp-tags">
        <span class="rp-tag">User Research</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Personas</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Information Architecture</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Product UI</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Usability Testing</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Front-end</span>
      </div>

      <div class="rp-ladder">

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">01 — Research</p>
          <h3>The struggle wasn't qualification. It was communication.</h3>
          <p>
            We started by listening — interviews and surveys with internationally educated
            healthcare workers. A clear pattern came back: not language alone, but
            confidence, cultural context, and the quiet fear of being misunderstood by a
            system that wasn't built with you in mind.
          </p>
          <p>
            That became <strong>Marco Dizon</strong>, our primary persona: a nurse from the
            Philippines with years of clinical experience who falters in Canadian
            interviews. Technically overqualified, emotionally underconfident. That
            combination was the design brief.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">02 — The brief</p>
          <h3>How do you build confidence without adding pressure?</h3>
          <p>
            Most interview prep tools are high-stakes simulations, so they amplify the
            anxiety they were meant to relieve. Marco needed a sandbox instead — a place to
            practice, fail safely, get specific feedback, and go again. The challenge wasn't
            building the app. It was designing the emotional experience: every button, every
            feedback message, every loading state had to say <em>you're improving</em>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">03 — Structure</p>
          <h3>Three pillars, then an architecture around Marco's journey.</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Growth</strong>, <strong>confidence</strong> and
            <strong>communication</strong> guided every call. The information architecture
            followed the journey: understand the role he's targeting, practice with tailored
            questions, get concrete feedback, review and iterate.
          </p>
          <p>
            The timeline was tight, so we prioritised the practice-interview flow — the one
            Marco would return to most — and accepted that everything else would follow it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">04 — The decision</p>
          <h3>Data, not judgment.</h3>
          <p>
            The system detects filler words in real time. The obvious move was to flag
            them — tell someone the instant they say "um."
          </p>
          <div class="rp-decision">
            <p class="rp-decision-k">The call I'd defend</p>
            <p>
              I didn't. Someone rehearsing for a high-stakes interview does not need a
              machine scoring them mid-sentence. So the detection became a pattern they read
              back afterward, in their own speech, next to STAR-method scaffolding — and the
              timer was split, counting up while they practise and down only when they ask
              for a real mock.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">05 — The test</p>
          <h3>Twelve users, and one thing they told us to fix.</h3>
          <p>
            Ease of use came back at 8 out of 10. What they wanted changed was the feedback
            flow, so we redesigned it before launch rather than shipping and promising to
            revisit it.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="rp-media-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="rp-media">
            <img src="/PersonaPrimary.webp" alt="Marco Dizon, the primary persona — a nurse from the Philippines with years of clinical experience, profiled with goals, frustrations and interview anxieties." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="rp-caption">Marco Dizon — built from the interviews, not from a template.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-media">
            <img src="/sitemap.webp" alt="Onward's information architecture and user flows, mapping the journey from awareness through practice to review." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="rp-caption">The architecture, mapped to his journey rather than to our feature list.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rp-stats">
        <div>
          <div class="rp-stat-n">8/10</div>
          <div class="rp-stat-l">Ease of use, 12 users</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-stat-n">4</div>
          <div class="rp-stat-l">Months to shipped</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-stat-n">200+</div>
          <div class="rp-stat-l">Presented to, live</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rp-media">
        <img src="/ONWARD-main.webp" alt="The Onward dashboard — practice and mock interview entry points, saved resumes, and interview history." loading="lazy" />
      </div>

      <p class="rp-note">
        Onward was pitched to VANTEC Network angel investors and presented live to a room
        of professionals, investors and government officials. It was a team project; the
        product design, the design system, the research and the pitch were mine.
      </p>

      <div class="rp-cta-links">
        <a href="https://onward-p2.vercel.app/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">see it running &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/onward.html" class="main-link">read the full case study &#8599;</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="rp-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════ PROJECT 02 — FEEDS ═══════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="feeds" class="rp-section">
      <p class="rp-section-label">Project 02 — Design system in production</p>
      <div class="rp-project-head">
        <h2 class="rp-project-title">Feeds</h2>
        <span class="rp-meta">Brand Identity · Product UI · 2026</span>
      </div>
      <p class="rp-lede">
        A self-hosted reader and digest engine for security analysts. I didn't write the
        engine — an engineer I work with did — but more or less everything you can see,
        I made.
      </p>

      <div class="rp-tags">
        <span class="rp-tag">Design System</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Guidelines</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Product UI</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Accessibility</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Front-end</span>
        <span class="rp-tag">Client Collaboration</span>
      </div>

      <div class="rp-spec">
        <div>
          <div class="rp-spec-n">11</div>
          <div class="rp-spec-l">Pages of<br>guidelines</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-spec-n">5</div>
          <div class="rp-spec-l">Colours, with<br>usage ratios</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-spec-n">AA</div>
          <div class="rp-spec-l">Contrast floor,<br>specified</div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-spec-n">4</div>
          <div class="rp-spec-l">Surfaces it<br>shipped into</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rp-ladder">

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">01 — The system</p>
          <h3>Specified down to the ratio.</h3>
          <p>
            A four-point spark held between two curved strokes, and a wordmark set in
            lowercase Bricolage Grotesque — never capitalised, never reset. Five colours,
            each with the proportion of a page it should occupy, and an AA contrast floor
            written in rather than assumed. Delivered as an eleven-page document to the
            developer building the product.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">02 — Into the product</p>
          <h3>I applied it myself, and took the code review.</h3>
          <p>
            The interface, the icon set, the Smart Inbox navigation — written in CSS and
            JavaScript, inside his repository, reviewed like anyone else's. Some of the UX
            calls came out of that: moving Run Audit inside the digest card, simplifying its
            toggle, and fixing empty-list CSS bugs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rp-step">
          <p class="rp-step-n">03 — Everywhere else</p>
          <h3>The same system, off the screen.</h3>
          <p>
            A reskinned digest email, a rebranded marketing site, and a live launch booth —
            printed table runners, die-cut mark stickers in four colourways, and two reels
            looping on the screens behind the table.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <div class="rp-media-grid">
        <div>
          <div class="rp-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-ui-articles.webp" alt="The Feeds article list after the brand refresh, showing inline CVE, malware and actor chips extracted locally at view time." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="rp-caption">The product UI after the refresh — I wrote this front-end.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="rp-media">
            <img src="/feeds/feeds-booth.webp" alt="The Feeds booth during the showcase — table runners carrying the mark and tagline, a laptop looping the brand identity reel, and the live product site on the screen behind." loading="lazy" />
          </div>
          <p class="rp-caption">The launch booth, printed from the same brand kit.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="rp-note">
        Worth being precise about ownership: the Go engine, the architecture and the core
        product logic are the engineer's. The identity, the guidelines, the applied
        front-end, the email, the marketing site and the launch are mine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="rp-divider"></div>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════ ABOUT / CTA ═══════════════════════════════ -->
    <section id="about" class="rp-section" style="min-height: 90vh;">
      <p class="rp-section-label">About</p>
      <h2 class="rp-h2">Where I am,<br>plainly.</h2>
      <p class="rp-lede">
        Vancouver-based. I hold a Bachelor of Creative
        Industries and a Diploma in Digital Design &amp; Development, both from BCIT.
        Before that I studied cognitive science, philosophy and digital humanities at the
        University of Toronto — which is where I picked up the habit of asking how people
        actually think before deciding what they should see.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-lede">
        I'm earlier than the two years your posting asks for, and I'd rather name that than
        inflate it. What I have instead is work that shipped and got used: a product tested
        with real users and redesigned on what they said, a design system living in someone
        else's repository, and clients whose businesses depended on the thing being right.
      </p>
      <p class="rp-lede">
        I use Claude daily in my own practice — for research, for building, for
        pressure-testing my own decisions. An AI-forward studio isn't a leap for me; it's
        already what my workflow looks like.
      </p>

      <div class="rp-cta-links">
        <a href="mailto:hello@rawanaladdin.com" class="main-link">hello@rawanaladdin.com &#8599;</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/rawan-aladdin" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="main-link">linkedin &#8599;</a>
        <a href="/projects.html" class="main-link">full portfolio &#8599;</a>
      </div>

      <p class="rp-footnote">
        Made for Robots &amp; Pencils — August 2026. Built in Astro, by me.
      </p>
    </section>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>
